TI  - Fetal cervical sarcoma: Comparison between virtual and real fetal bronchoscopy.
PG  - 1478-1481
LID - 10.1002/jcu.23806 [doi]
AB  - Fetal cervical tumors are a life-threatening condition if proper diagnosis and 
      perinatal care are inadequate. The most common causes of fetal cervical masses 
      are teratomas and lymphangiomas. Less common tumors may obstruct the fetal airway 
      due to cervical compression. In this case report, a fetus with a cervical sarcoma 
      was evaluated by ultrasound and MRI. The prenatal imaging characteristics of the 
      tumor were described, virtual navigation of the fetal airway was performed and 
      compared with fetal bronchoscopy to evaluate the consistency of the tumor. 
      Neonatal outcome was also described.
