TI  - [Metastatic Lung Tumor with Bronchial Cast Shadow in Which Intraoperative 
      Bronchoscopy Was Useful During Robotic Surgery].
PG  - 1080-1084
AB  - A man in his 60s, who had undergone surgery for rectal cancer, liver metastases, 
      and lung metastasis, had a past history of myocardial infarction and ventricular 
      fibrillation with reduced cardiac functions. He was referred to our department 
      because of a pulmonary nodule shadow in the S2 right upper lobe and a bronchial 
      cast shadow along the B2 bronchus. Robot-assisted thoracoscopic right S2 
      segmentectomy was performed and intraoperative bronchoscopy revealed a polyp-like 
      tumor within B2a. Then, B2a and B2b were resected and the tumor was removed into 
      the surgical field. It was confirmed that there was no residue using a 
      bronchoscope, and the bronchus was cut on the proximal side of B2 using an 
      automatic suturing device. The final pathological diagnosis was rectal cancer 
      with lung metastasis, and the endobronchial tumor showed necrotic tissue and 
      bronchial stumps were negative.
