TI  - Making the case for multidisciplinary pediatric aerodigestive programs.
PG  - 3620-3626
LID - 10.3748/wjg.v28.i28.3620 [doi]
AB  - Multidisciplinary pediatric aerodigestive centers have been proposed to address 
      the needs of children with complex multi-system problems affecting the 
      respiratory and upper gastrointestinal tracts. The setup of a multidisciplinary 
      service allows for the complex coordination needed between different 
      subspecialties. This allows for rapid communication and family-centered decision 
      making and agreement on further diagnostic and/or therapeutic next steps such as 
      offering triple endoscopy when indicated. Triple endoscopy entails performing 
      rigid upper airway assessment, flexible bronchoscopy and upper gastrointestinal 
      endoscopy and has been linked to reduced time to diagnosis/treatment, reduced 
      costs and anesthesia exposure. This review summarizes the available literature on 
      the structure and benefits of multidisciplinary pediatric aerodigestive services.
