VI  - 166
DP  - 2022 Jan 12
TI  - [Is it difficult-to-treat asthma? Or another diagnosis?].
LID - D5948 [pii]
AB  - BACKGROUND: When suspected of difficult-to-treat asthma, it is important to 
      detect causal factors. Central airway malignancy should also be excluded in this 
      group. CASE DESCRIPTION: We describe the case of a 23-year-old woman who came to 
      the emergency room for shortness of breath and was subsequently admitted to the 
      intensive care unit for respiratory failure, indicated by an asthma exacerbation. 
      At an outpatient visit for dyspnea complaints, now with a stridor on physical 
      examination, a chest CT was performed, which showed an occlusive lesion. 
      Bronchoscopy turned out to be an endobronchial tumor. PA showed that it was a 
      primary pulmonary myxoid sarcoma. CONCLUSION: The case above shows why it is 
      important to consider rare pathologies, such as a central airway tumor, in asthma 
      that is difficult to treat. A thorough history and physical examination is an 
      important first step, especially the presence of a stridor.
