TI  - Oblique Carinal End-to-end Anastomosis for Pig Bronchus in Organ Donor and Lung 
      Transplant Recipient.
PG  - e195-e197
LID - S0003-4975(21)00986-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2021.05.029 [doi]
AB  - Pig bronchi are rare anomalies in which the right upper lobe bronchus originates 
      above the carina. During surgery this can lead to technical challenges associated 
      with the bronchial anastomosis, especially during lung transplantation. We herein 
      report the case of a combined liver-lung transplantation with a pig bronchus in 
      both the organ donor and transplant recipient. In both cases the bronchi 
      originated slightly above the level of the carina facilitating an oblique 
      resection and a single tracheobronchial anastomosis with a running suture. 
      Follow-up bronchoscopy showed a completely healed anastomosis with no evidence of 
      malacia or stenosis.
