TI  - Eosinophilic plastic bronchitis: Case series and review of the literature.
PG  - 3023-3031
LID - 10.1002/ppul.26650 [doi]
AB  - Plastic bronchitis is a term used to describe group of life-threatening disorders 
      characterized by the presence of large obstructing casts in the airways. 
      Eosinophilic plastic bronchitis is a subtype of plastic bronchitis that occurs 
      mainly in children and has not been well-described in the literature. Patients 
      may have a history of asthma or atopy, but many do not. They often present with 
      cough and wheezing, and frequently have complete collapse of one lung seen on 
      imaging. The severity of presentation varies depending on the location of the 
      casts, ranging from mild symptoms to severe airway obstruction and death. 
      Bronchoscopy is often required to both diagnose and treat this condition. A 
      variety of medical therapies have been used, although no formal studies have 
      evaluated their efficacy. Symptoms may resolve after initial cast removal, but in 
      some patients, cast formation recurs. Here, we report a case series of nine 
      patients with eosinophilic plastic bronchitis and review the existing literature 
      of this condition.
