TI  - [Cerebral air embolism: A rare complication of flexible fiberoptic bronchoscopy].
PG  - 662-665
LID - S0761-8425(20)30217-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.rmr.2020.07.003 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Cerebral air embolism is a rare complication of flexible fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy. It is a serious, life-threatening complication. The treatment 
      consists of hyperbaric oxygen therapy. CASE REPORT: We report the case of 
      cerebral air embolism that occurred in an 80-year-old woman after a flexible 
      bronchial fibroscopy with bronchial spur biopsies. The patient showed 
      neurological signs after the procedure. The brain CT-scan found disseminated air 
      emboli. The progress was fatal in the absence of specific treatment, taking 
      account of the context, the patient's comorbidities and the wishes of the family. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Cerebral air embolism is a serious complication that can occur 
      during a bronchial biopsy even though this complication is rare.
