TI  - Case report: tracheoesophageal fistula secondary to post-intubation tracheomegaly 
      in a tetanus patient.
PG  - 4922-4925
LID - 10.21037/apm-19-681 [doi]
AB  - Tracheomegaly and tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) may be complicated within 
      12-200 days (with a mean of 43 days) of mechanical ventilation but rare in 
      short-term intubation. Here we present a case of TEF secondary to post-intubation 
      tracheomegaly in a tetanus patient. A 49-year-old male was admitted to the 
      emergency room (ER) and diagnosed with tetanus. He became intubated and 
      mechanically ventilated, but showed over-inflation of the endotracheal tube cuff 
      on X-ray and chest computed tomography since the 8th day. After extubation, the 
      patient had severe coughing during eating. Fiberoptic bronchoscopy and 
      gastroscopy demonstrated a TEF located at the anterior wall of the esophagus. 
      Esophageal exclusion and jejunostomy were performed to heal the fistula. The 
      recurrent and uncontrollable muscular rigidity and spasms might be the main cause 
      early tracheomegaly and TEF. Short-term intubation induced TEF should be aware of 
      in specific patients. Both cuff pressure and cuff volume should be monitored to 
      minimize tracheoesophageal injuries in such cases.
