TI  - Interventional treatment of giant tracheal lymphoma under rigid bronchoscopy: A 
      case report and literature review.
PG  - e36736
LID - 10.1097/MD.0000000000036736 [doi]
LID - e36736
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Lymphoma can appear in all parts of the body and present with 
      different symptoms. However, bronchial lymphoma is rare and can be misdiagnosed 
      as airway malignancy or lung disease.Patient: An older adult woman with tracheal 
      lymphoma experienced severe breathing difficulties, and chest computed tomography 
      indicated severe narrowing of the airway. She did not respond to repeated 
      antibiotic treatment, and she was eventually diagnosed with lymphoma based on 
      pathology after surgical removal of the tumor. DIAGNOSIS: The patient received a 
      diagnosis of thoracic tracheal stenosis due to intratracheal inflammatory 
      granulomatous lesions or a tumor. INTERVENTIONS: Treatment involved the use of a 
      high-frequency electrotome, freezing, and argon plasma coagulation. OUTCOMES: The 
      patient reported improvements in dyspnea, cough, and other symptoms after the 
      operation. The pathological results confirmed follicular lymphoma. Reexamination 
      by fiberbronchoscopy indicated that the degree of stenosis in the middle and 
      upper tracheal segments was significantly reduced following interventional 
      therapy. CONCLUSION: Endoscopic interventional therapy can be an effective 
      treatment for tracheal lymphoma.
