TI  - Successful Resection of a Primary Dedifferentiated Tracheal Liposarcoma Causing 
      Tracheal Stenosis.
PG  - 719-722
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.6696-20 [doi]
AB  - A 68-year-old woman was admitted with a persistent cough and dyspnea that had 
      persisted for 4 months prior. Chest computed tomography revealed a tumor 
      protruding from the membranous portion of the trachea. She underwent tumor 
      resection via rigid and flexible bronchoscopy to relieve the symptoms and obtain 
      a diagnosis. After the procedure, she was diagnosed with tracheal liposarcoma. 
      Three months after the procedure, she underwent complete surgical tumor 
      resection. Liposarcoma is a mesenchymal tumor that usually develops in the 
      extremities and the retroperitoneum. Tracheal liposarcoma is extremely rare. To 
      the best of our knowledge, this is only the second reported case.
