VI  - 70
IP  - 1
DP  - 2021
TI  - [BRONCHIAL ASTHMA WITH VOCAL CORD DYSFUNCTION DIFFERENTIATED FROM SEVERE ASTHMA 
      BY BRONCHOSCOPY].
PG  - 33-38
LID - 10.15036/arerugi.70.33 [doi]
AB  - Although an important cause of vocal cord dysfunction (VCD) is psychogenic 
      reaction, VCD may be associated with severe asthma and must be distinguished from 
      the disease. A 30-years-old woman was admitted to our hospital with dyspnea 
      despite treatment for asthma. Inspiratory stridor and expiratory wheezes were 
      noted, and neck and chest computed tomography showed normal airways and lungs. 
      Fractional exhaled nitric oxide levels were also normal. Pulmonary function test 
      with a flow-volume loop curve showed normal expiratory loop with flattening of 
      the inspiratory loop after methacholine inhalation. During the attack, 
      bronchoscopy revealed the vocal cord closing with stridor during the inspiratory 
      phase. Therefore, the patient was diagnosed with VCD. The dyspnea improved with 
      respiratory rehabilitation and pursed-lip breathing. VCD should be considered in 
      the differential diagnosis of intractable severe asthma. In this case, 
      bronchoscopy and bronchial inhalation challenge with methacholine helped in the 
      diagnosis.
