TI  - Augmented fluoroscopic bronchoscopy (AFB) versus percutaneous computed 
      tomography-guided dye localization for thoracoscopic resection of small lung 
      nodules: a propensity-matched study.
PG  - 5393-5401
LID - 10.1007/s00464-019-07334-4 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Dye localization is a useful method for the resection of 
      unidentifiable small pulmonary lesions. This study compares the transbronchial 
      route with augmented fluoroscopic bronchoscopy (AFB) and conventional 
      transthoracic CT-guided methods for preoperative dye localization in 
      thoracoscopic surgery. METHODS: Between April 2015 and March 2019, a total of 231 
      patients with small pulmonary lesions who received preoperative dye localization 
      via AFB or percutaneous CT-guided technique were enrolled in the study. A 
      propensity-matched analysis, incorporating preoperative variables, was used to 
      compare localization and surgical outcomes between the two groups. RESULTS: After 
      matching, a total of 90 patients in the AFB group (N = 30) and CT-guided group 
      (N = 60) were selected for analysis. No significant difference was noted in the 
      demographic data between both the groups. Dye localization was successfully 
      performed in 29 patients (96.7%) and 57 patients (95%) with AFB and CT-guided 
      method, respectively. The localization duration (24.1 ± 8.3 vs. 21.4 ± 12.5 min, 
      p = 0.297) and equivalent dose of radiation exposure (3.1 ± 1.5 vs. 
      2.5 ± 2.0 mSv, p = 0.130) were comparable in both the groups. No major 
      procedure-related complications occurred in either group; however, a higher rate 
      of pneumothorax (0 vs. 16.7%, p = 0.029) and focal intrapulmonary hemorrhage (3.3 
      vs. 26.7%, p = 0.008) was noted in the CT-guided group. CONCLUSION: AFB dye 
      marking is an effective alternative for the preoperative localization of small 
      pulmonary lesions, with a lower risk of procedure-related complications than the 
      conventional CT-guided method.
