VI  - 48
IP  - 4
DP  - 2020 Apr
TI  - Mycobacterium tuberculosis diagnosed promptly by bronchial brushing cytology in 
      an immunocompetent patient.
PG  - 368-370
LID - 10.1002/dc.24367 [doi]
AB  - Respiratory cytology plays an important role in the diagnosis of lower 
      respiratory tract infection. The timely diagnosis of pulmonary tuberculosis (TB) 
      can be very challenging due to the nonspecific cytomorphologic features and 
      limited number of organisms, especially in the immunocompetent patients. Here, we 
      reported a case of TB diagnosed promptly by bronchial brushing cytology in a 
      51-year-old immunocompetent patient. She presented with a 4 cm fungating lesion 
      involving right lower lobe of the lung and mediastinal lymphadenopathy with an 
      initial concern for malignancy. Bronchial brushing showed scattered acute 
      inflammatory cells in the background of necrosis. A cell block was prepared and 
      acid-fast bacilli (AFB)-positive organisms were identified. Subsequent polymerase 
      chain reaction (PCR) performed on the sputum detected Mycobacterium tuberculosis. 
      This case highlights the importance of recognizing the cytomorphology of TB from 
      a bronchial brushing specimen; and also emphasizes the potential utility of the 
      cell block from respiratory cytology in the diagnosis of TB.
