TI  - Diagnostic performance of the bronchial aspirate collected before and after the 
      bronchial biopsy in patients with pulmonary neoplasia with endobronchial lesion.
PG  - 13-15
LID - S0025-7753(18)30650-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.medcli.2018.10.019 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES: In patients with lung cancer (LC) and endobronchial 
      lesion, the optimal sequence for collecting bronchial aspirate, before (BASpre) 
      or after the biopsy (BASpost) is not yet established. The aim of this study was 
      to compare the diagnostic performance of BASpre with BASpost. MATERIALS AND 
      METHODS: Retrospective study of patients with LC and endobronchial lesion 
      undergoing bronchoscopy with bronchial biopsy and BASpre and BASpost samples. The 
      diagnostic performance of both techniques was calculated. RESULTS: A total of 144 
      patients were included. BASpre was diagnostic in 24 (16.7%) cases and BASpost in 
      33 (22.9%) (Chi-squared P<0.009). The number of cases in which it was the only 
      diagnostic method was identical: Two for BASpre and two cases for BASpost. 
      CONCLUSIONS: In patients with LC and endobronchial lesion, BASpost is diagnosed 
      in a higher percentage of cases than BASpre. This difference does not affect the 
      overall diagnostic performance of bronchoscopy as the number of times in which 
      either is the only diagnostic procedure is identical.
