TI  - Acute hypoxemia due to lung collapse in COVID-19: the role of therapeutic 
      bronchoscopy.
PG  - 448-450
LID - 10.5603/ARM.a2021.0009 [doi]
AB  - Bronchoscopy is an aerosol-generating procedure and involves a high risk of 
      transmission of SARS-CoV-2 to health care workers. There are very few indications 
      for performing bronchoscopy in a patient with confirmed COVID-19. These include 
      atelectasis, foreign body aspiration, and suspected superinfection in 
      immunocompromised patients. Proper use of standard personal protective equipment 
      is mandatory to reduce the risk of transmission to health care workers. In this 
      article, we describe a case of acute lung collapse in a 16-year-old boy with 
      cerebral palsy who was infected with COVID-19. This patient responded to 
      therapeutic bronchoscopy and had complete resolution of lung collapse within 24 
      hours of the procedure.
