TI  - Foreign body aspiration in a child with cardiac arrest.
PG  - 29-31
LID - 10.1097/01.JAA.0000615480.69807.99 [doi]
AB  - Foreign body aspiration is a true medical emergency and is the fifth most common 
      cause of mortality due to unintentional injury in the United States. Children who 
      have aspirated a foreign body may present with a variety of symptoms, from mild 
      irritation and cough to respiratory failure. Treatment is removal of the foreign 
      body, often via rigid bronchoscopy. Clinicians must have a high degree of 
      suspicion to ensure a foreign body aspiration is not missed on initial 
      presentation.
