TI  - Temporary Tracheal Stenting After Pulmonary Artery Sling Repair in a Newborn.
PG  - NP69-NP71
LID - 10.1177/2150135117713740 [doi]
AB  - Pulmonary artery sling (PAS) is a rare disease frequently associated with severe 
      malacia and stenosis of the trachea. We present a two-day-old newborn that 
      underwent urgent surgery for PAS and needed prolonged respiratory support 
      afterward. Temporary airway stenting above the level of the tracheal bifurcation 
      was performed five days after surgery to overcome severe airway obstruction 
      caused by tracheomalacia and laceration of the tracheal mucosa after diagnostic 
      bronchoscopy. Two days after the procedure, the child could be extubated and 
      after two weeks the stent was removed without complications. Temporary tracheal 
      stenting after PAS repair can be an effective therapeutic strategy in newborns 
      with tracheal obstruction and allows timely weaning from ventilator support.
